Scope: SPEC §2a (design tokens + type floor), ruling U-2026-09-04j (renderError
in accounts/whatif/majorexpenses handlers -> token classes; emitter_coverage
check 9; error_probe check 10; darken successRateTextClass/BarClass tiers).
Standard: budget2 ACCESSIBILITY.md (17 points) + WCAG 2.2 AA.

1. INDEPENDENT ORACLE RE-RUN (not trusting the worker's log)
   First run (bash .swarm/tier3/U6/accept.sh, own invocation, not the
   worker's oracle.4.log): FAILED at checks 7 and 10 with
   net::ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED against the verify server on :8198. Traced
   the cause before accepting/rejecting: at that moment a leftover
   CPU-heavy `go build` from an earlier fail-end-validation run
   (scratchpad/u6-failend, unrelated to this attempt, visible in `ps aux`)
   was still consuming ~200% CPU, starving the verify-server's own
   `go build` inside whatif-verify.sh and destabilizing it between the
   health check and the first page load. Confirmed the job had exited,
   re-ran the FULL oracle a second time with no contention:
     CHECK 0-10: all PASS. SUMMARY: 11 passed, 0 failed. ORACLE PASS.
   No stray server left on :8198 afterward (`ss -ltnp`, `ls /tmp/budget2-
   whatif-verify-8198` both empty). This matches the worker's
   oracle.4.log exactly. Verdict: the failing first run was environmental
   flakiness, not a defect; the clean second run is the operative result.

2. renderError TOKEN CONVERSION (live-traced, not just diffed)
   `git diff master` on internal/handlers/{accounts,whatif,majorexpenses}/
   handlers.go: each renderError() converted from
   `bg-red-50 dark:bg-red-900/30 border-red-200 dark:border-red-800` /
   `text-red-700 dark:text-red-300` / `text-red-600 dark:text-red-400` to
   `bg-negative-soft border-negative` / `text-negative` / `text-body-sm
   text-negative` — no dark: twin needed (CSS custom properties flip per
   theme). Traced all three live against scripts/whatif-verify.sh (own
   throwaway copy, ports 8199/8198, never :8080):
     POST /accounts/no-such-account/anchor (anchor_date/anchor_amount) ->
       HTTP 404, body is exactly renderError's token markup.
     POST /whatif/expense (name=&amount=not-a-number) -> HTTP 400,
       bg-negative-soft/border-negative/text-negative/text-body-sm present.
     DELETE /major-expenses/no-such-expense -> HTTP 500, same token set.
   No `red-*` literal in any of the three responses. error_probe.js
   (oracle check 10) independently confirms non-transparent background,
   visible border, and zero axe color-contrast violations for all three
   cases in both light and dark themes.

3. error_probe.js FIELD-NAME FIX (scope-discipline question, item 4)
   Traced both the OLD probe's field names and the NEW ones against the
   live server:
     date=...&amount=100          -> HTTP 200, a DIFFERENT inline
       validation-error fragment (role="alert", already token-styled
       separately) — renderError's not-found path is NEVER reached.
     anchor_date=...&anchor_amount=100 -> HTTP 404, renderError's actual
       body verbatim.
   This confirms the worker's stated reason (old names hit the "required
   fields" branch before ever calling renderError) is factually correct,
   and the fix makes check 10 exercise the intended code path instead of
   passing vacuously against an unrelated branch. Judgment: STRENGTHENED,
   not weakened. Legitimate oracle fix, in scope (the case is the oracle's
   own, not the app).

4. successRateTextClass DARKENING (contrast math + live trace)
   render.go: lime-600->700, yellow-600->700, orange-600->700 on the light
   side only (dark: variants unchanged, already passing on a dark ground),
   with contrast-ratio citations in comments (3.09/2.94/3.56:1 failing ->
   4.99/4.92/5.18:1 passing). These three classes are used only inside
   /whatif's "risk" and "strategies" tab panels (monte-carlo.html,
   historical-backtest.html, tax-optimizer.html), which are `hidden` by
   default and only shown via whatif-tabs.js's activateTab().
   FINDING: the reconstructed render_probe.js (oracle check 7) opens
   `<details>` elements but does NOT click what-if tabs or open the
   dashboard's HTMX-loaded modals (kpi-detail, kpi-month-detail,
   major-expense-drilldown all load into empty containers via
   htmx.ajax(), so they are absent from the DOM entirely at page load,
   not merely `hidden`). This is the exact gap the attempt-3 addendum in
   SPEC.md §10 required attempt 4's oracle to close ("must activate every
   tab and open every modal before measuring"); the reconstruction after
   the worktree loss did not carry that requirement into render_probe.js,
   so check 7's "9 pages x 2 themes" claim does not actually reach the
   very code this attempt was scoped to fix.
   Did NOT stop at the log — independently drove the real app to close
   this gap myself:
     - Clicked all 5 what-if tabs (cashflow/risk/taxes/strategies/overview)
       in both themes and ran axe color-contrast on the full document
       after each activation: zero violations every time, both themes.
     - Triggered all 4 KPI-detail modal types (income/expenses/living/
       healthcare) via window.openKPIDetail() in both themes: zero axe
       color-contrast violations.
     - Triggered kpi-month-detail (openKPIMonthDetail) in both themes:
       zero violations.
     - major-expense-drilldown.html's diff is a 2-line mechanical
       text-red-600/dark:text-red-400 -> text-negative swap, identical to
       the token already verified clean elsewhere; not independently
       re-tested live (no seed data exercised the trigger on dashboard),
       judged low-risk by construction.
   Net: the darkened tiers and the token conversions are ACTUALLY clean in
   both themes on real rendered content, verified by me, independent of
   the oracle's blind spot. This is a PASS on the accessibility facts, but
   the oracle process itself has a real, reproducible gap that should be
   fixed before it is trusted again for tab/modal-gated content (see
   OBSERVATIONS).

